<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>





<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 16 Practice</title>

    <p >
        <em>Given a statement to be proven using a proof by contradiction, identify the assumptions to be made and the statements to be proven.

        </em>
    </p>

   <paragraphs>
           <title>Need to know</title>
   
           <p>
               <ul>
                   <li>
                       <p>
                           How to negate conditional statements (e.g.
                           <q>if...then...</q> statements).
                       </p>
                   </li>
   
                   <li>
                       <p>
                           What to assume at the start of a proof by contradiction.
                       </p>
                   </li>
   
                   <li>
                       <p>
                           What a contradiction is at the end of a proof by contradiction, and examples of some common contradictions.
                       </p>
                   </li>
               </ul>
           </p>
        </paragraphs>
   
       <paragraphs>
           <title>Practice Questions</title>
   
          <exercise>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Weekly Practice 11 (just what you assume and make note of the contradictions you arrive at)
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                         Worksheet page 92
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The Learning Target 14 review (you need to know how to negate statements!)
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                      For each of the following statements, state what you would assume in a proof by contradiction.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>For all <m>n\in\mathbb{Z}</m>, if <m>n</m> is odd, then <m>n</m> cannot be
                                        expressed as the sum of <m>3</m> even integers.
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                   <p>
                    Assume there exists <m>n\in\mathbb{Z}</m> such that <m>n</m> is odd and <m>n</m> can 
                    be expressed as the sum of <m>3</m> integers.
                   </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                         For all <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>a\neq 0</m> and <m>b\neq 0</m> then
                                                  <m>(a+b)^{2}\neq a^{2} + b^{2}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Assume there exist <m>a,b\in\mathbb{R}</m> such that <m>a\neq 0</m>, <m>b\neq 0</m>
                        and <m>(a+b)^2 = a^2+b^2</m>.
                                   </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       For all <m>x,y\in\mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are positive,
                                               <m>x-4y&lt;y-3x</m> and <m>3x&gt;2y</m> then <m>12x^{2}+10y^{2}&lt;24xy</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Assume there exists <m>x,y\in\mathbb{R}</m> such that  <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are positive,
                                               <m>x-4y&lt;y-3x</m> and <m>3x&gt;2y</m> and <m>12x^{2}+10y^{2}\geq 24xy</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            


            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                         For every real number <m>x</m>, there exists a
                                       real number <m>y</m> such that <m>x + y = 0</m>. 
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Assume that there exists a real number <m>x</m> such that for all real numbers
                        <m>y</m>, <m>x+y\neq 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
      
   
                  
                         

             </exercise>

             <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the goal in a proof by contradiction?
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        To reach a statement that is obviously false (in other words a contradiction).
                    </p>
                </solution>
             </exercise>

             <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Name at least 4 examples of contradictions.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Lots of answers here, e.g., <m>0=1</m>, <m>2</m> is odd, <m>n\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>n^2 &lt; 0</m>,
                        <m>4</m> is prime, etc.
                    </p>
                </solution>
             </exercise>
       </paragraphs>

        </handout>